            <ArticleTitle>Reoperation for a patient with Starr-Edwards aortic valve malfunction due to pannus formation twenty-nine years after implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>437-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a re-do case of severe aortic valve stenosis due to pannus formation 29 years after an aortic Starr-Edwards caged-ball valve implantation. A huge shelf of calcified and thick pannus tissue below the valve had reduced the already small orifice by at least a third in surface area. The explanted Starr-Edwards valve revealed no mechanical or structural failure. Early detection and treatment of pannus outgrowth is essential in order to prevent life-threatening prosthetic valve malfunctions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
